WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.455
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Proficiency.

2
00:00:05.755 --> 00:00:08.804
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.104 --> 00:00:18.409
Unit eighteen: Communication in the Digital Age. In this unit, you will analyse how digital media change communication.

4
00:00:19.417 --> 00:00:20.371
filter bubble.

5
00:00:21.071 --> 00:00:26.554
filter bubble means an online space where you only see views similar to your own.

6
00:00:26.854 --> 00:00:29.585
Filter bubbles can reinforce prejudices.

7
00:00:30.285 --> 00:00:33.367
Your turn. Say: filter bubble.

8
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:37.068
filter bubble.

9
00:00:38.083 --> 00:00:39.618
algorithmic curation.

10
00:00:40.318 --> 00:00:44.512
algorithmic curation means computer selection of the content you see.

11
00:00:44.812 --> 00:00:47.543
Algorithmic curation decides your news feed.

12
00:00:48.243 --> 00:00:51.697
Your turn. Say: algorithmic curation.

13
00:00:54.397 --> 00:00:56.443
algorithmic curation.

14
00:00:57.450 --> 00:00:58.556
virality.

15
00:00:59.256 --> 00:01:03.090
virality means the tendency of content to spread rapidly online.

16
00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:06.434
Virality rewards emotion over accuracy.

17
00:01:07.134 --> 00:01:09.995
Your turn. Say: virality.

18
00:01:12.695 --> 00:01:13.731
virality.

19
00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:16.020
digital etiquette.

20
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:21.169
digital etiquette means polite behaviour in online communication.

21
00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:25.024
Good digital etiquette includes not shouting in capitals.

22
00:01:25.724 --> 00:01:28.620
Your turn. Say: digital etiquette.

23
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:32.612
digital etiquette.

24
00:01:33.617 --> 00:01:34.885
online discourse.

25
00:01:35.585 --> 00:01:39.767
online discourse means public discussion taking place on the internet.

26
00:01:40.067 --> 00:01:43.320
Online discourse has become increasingly polarised.

27
00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.520
Your turn. Say: online discourse.

28
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:51.709
online discourse.

29
00:01:52.717 --> 00:01:53.962
cancel culture.

30
00:01:54.662 --> 00:02:00.098
cancel culture means the practice of publicly rejecting someone after offensive behaviour.

31
00:02:00.398 --> 00:02:02.966
Debates about cancel culture divide opinion.

32
00:02:03.666 --> 00:02:06.551
Your turn. Say: cancel culture.

33
00:02:09.251 --> 00:02:10.414
cancel culture.

34
00:02:11.417 --> 00:02:13.196
parasocial relationship.

35
00:02:13.896 --> 00:02:19.715
parasocial relationship means a one-sided emotional bond with a media personality.

36
00:02:20.015 --> 00:02:23.965
Fans may form parasocial relationships with influencers.

37
00:02:24.665 --> 00:02:28.722
Your turn. Say: parasocial relationship.

38
00:02:31.422 --> 00:02:33.259
parasocial relationship.

39
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.681
attention span.

40
00:02:36.381 --> 00:02:40.110
attention span means the length of time you can concentrate.

41
00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:43.280
Short videos may reduce our attention span.

42
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.294
Your turn. Say: attention span.

43
00:02:49.994 --> 00:02:51.286
attention span.

44
00:02:52.283 --> 00:02:54.469
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:54.769 --> 00:02:59.906
Ms. Sarah, I read a long article about filter bubbles. Can I summarise it?

46
00:03:01.217 --> 00:03:03.228
Please do, in your own words.

47
00:03:04.517 --> 00:03:10.824
In essence, it argues that algorithmic curation narrows what we see, so we rarely meet opposing views.

48
00:03:12.117 --> 00:03:15.071
Good paraphrase. What evidence did it give?

49
00:03:16.383 --> 00:03:21.448
Put simply, studies show that emotional posts achieve greater virality than balanced ones.

50
00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.007
And the conclusion?

51
00:03:25.317 --> 00:03:30.985
To sum up, online discourse is becoming more polarised, and our attention span is shrinking.

52
00:03:32.283 --> 00:03:36.898
Excellent. You captured the main ideas without copying the author's words.

53
00:03:38.183 --> 00:03:41.251
Grammar focus: Paraphrase and summary.

54
00:03:41.551 --> 00:03:45.037
Paraphrasing means restating ideas in your own words.

55
00:03:45.337 --> 00:03:51.275
In essence, algorithms narrow what we see. The article argues that emotion drives virality.

56
00:03:51.575 --> 00:03:54.201
To sum up, online discourse is polarising.

57
00:03:54.501 --> 00:04:02.340
Good paraphrase changes structure and vocabulary while keeping meaning; always attribute ideas to their source.

58
00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:06.281
Now read and listen. Talking Past Each Other.

59
00:04:06.581 --> 00:04:10.961
Digital platforms have transformed how billions of people communicate.

60
00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.990
Algorithmic curation selects content based on what keeps users engaged.

61
00:04:18.283 --> 00:04:24.010
As a result, users may find themselves inside filter bubbles that confirm existing beliefs.

62
00:04:25.317 --> 00:04:30.265
Virality tends to reward emotional, provocative content over careful argument.

63
00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:36.510
Online discourse has consequently become faster, louder and more polarised.

64
00:04:37.817 --> 00:04:44.042
Phenomena such as cancel culture and parasocial relationships reveal new social dynamics.

65
00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:50.960
Researchers also question whether constant scrolling is shortening our attention span.

66
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:58.348
In short, digital communication connects us more than ever, yet may help us understand each other less.

67
00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:02.326
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:02.626 --> 00:05:06.506
An online space where you only see similar views is a blank.

69
00:05:06.806 --> 00:05:12.538
A, filter bubble. B, virality. C, attention span.

70
00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.933
The answer is A: filter bubble.

71
00:05:20.417 --> 00:05:22.765
Which phrase introduces a paraphrase?

72
00:05:23.065 --> 00:05:26.706
A, In essence. B, However. C, Because.

73
00:05:30.717 --> 00:05:33.053
The answer is A: In essence.

74
00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:36.677
Which phrase concludes a summary?

75
00:05:36.977 --> 00:05:41.200
A, To begin with. B, To sum up. C, Turning to.

76
00:05:45.183 --> 00:05:46.962
The answer is B: To sum up.

77
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:52.516
A one-sided emotional bond with a media personality is a blank.

78
00:05:52.816 --> 00:05:58.490
A, parasocial relationship. B, digital etiquette. C, cancel culture.

79
00:06:02.483 --> 00:06:06.131
The answer is A: parasocial relationship.

80
00:06:07.617 --> 00:06:11.683
According to the text, what does virality tend to reward?

81
00:06:11.983 --> 00:06:17.865
A, careful argument. B, emotional, provocative content. C, long articles.

82
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:25.591
The answer is B: emotional, provocative content.

83
00:06:27.083 --> 00:06:33.788
Now it's your turn. Part one: Summarise the main problem with filter bubbles, then give your opinion.

84
00:06:39.088 --> 00:06:43.966
Part two: Paraphrase first, then state and justify your view.

85
00:06:50.266 --> 00:07:04.717
In essence, filter bubbles show us only what we already believe, so we rarely meet challenging ideas. Put simply, they make us more certain and less informed. In my view, users should deliberately follow sources they disagree with.

86
00:07:05.017 --> 00:07:06.854
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:12.488
Great work today! You learned eight new words and paraphrase and summary.

88
00:07:12.788 --> 00:07:18.424
Today's value is online safety. Keep your password secret and be kind online.

89
00:07:18.724 --> 00:07:21.411
See you in Unit nineteen. Goodbye!

90
00:07:21.711 --> 00:07:24.421
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

